WEBVTT

00:04.000 --> 00:05.480
And now let's say that you ask me.

00:05.480 --> 00:05.960
You know what?

00:05.960 --> 00:11.000
I have this big table and I would like to speed up the queries using indexes.

00:11.000 --> 00:15.000
And my first question is going to be, what are you exactly doing with this table?

00:15.040 --> 00:20.960
Are you using this table to search for a text or are you doing like complex analysis with this table?

00:21.000 --> 00:27.360
And the reason why I'm asking this is that we have different indexes in databases for different purposes.

00:27.360 --> 00:31.800
So now let's have a quick look to the different types of indexes that we have in database.

00:31.840 --> 00:35.520
I divide the indexes in databases into three categories.

00:35.560 --> 00:41.400
The first one is by the structure, how the database is organizing and referencing the data.

00:41.400 --> 00:46.520
And here we have two types the clustered index and the non-clustered index.

00:46.520 --> 00:48.520
Those are very important to understand.

00:48.680 --> 00:51.160
Now we have another category for the indexes.

00:51.160 --> 00:53.360
We can divide them by the storage.

00:53.360 --> 00:59.520
And in this category we are talking about how the data is stored physically in the database.

00:59.520 --> 01:00.800
So we have two types.

01:00.800 --> 01:05.080
We have the row store index and the column store index.

01:05.120 --> 01:07.040
And the third type is the functions.

01:07.040 --> 01:08.720
And here we have two types.

01:08.880 --> 01:11.960
We have the unique index and the filtered index.

01:11.960 --> 01:15.000
Now each index type has its own strings.

01:15.000 --> 01:17.680
But as well there is always a trade off.

01:17.720 --> 01:19.680
Some might improve their read performance.

01:19.720 --> 01:23.840
The other one might improve the insert and update operations.

01:23.840 --> 01:27.720
So it's all about choosing the right type of index for the job.

01:27.720 --> 01:31.400
So now what we're going to do, we're going to go and deep dive into each of those types in order to

01:31.440 --> 01:34.680
understand how they work and how we can create them.

01:34.680 --> 01:37.360
And we will start with the first category, the structure.

01:37.360 --> 01:40.920
We have the clustered index and the non-clustered index.
